<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The utility stylesheet generator: design tokens from a config asset, a scanner that finds
            which utilities a project actually uses, and a VCSS stylesheet emitted into
            @layer utilities containing only those.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>css;utility;tailwind;design-tokens;ui;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />

        <!--
            The build step's MSBuild half, which is what makes a PackageReference to this assembly
            enough on its own: no scanner call, no manifest walk, no bootstrap of any kind in the
            consuming project.

            ⚠ **`buildTransitive/` and not `build/`, for the reason `Vixen.Ui.csproj` records at
            length.** NuGet imports `build/<id>.targets` only for a direct PackageReference, and
            nothing reaches this assembly directly: an application references `Vixen.Ui.Controls`,
            which depends on `Vixen.Ui`, which depends on this. Under `build/` the step was reachable
            only by a project that had gone looking for it.
        -->
        <None Include="build/Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="buildTransitive/" />

        <!--
            ⚠ **The tool is packed by path rather than by ProjectReference, and it has to be.**
            `Tools/Vixen.StyleGen` is where the step lives, and doc 00's layer rules forbid a Core
            assembly from referencing anything in Tools — a rule `CheckArchitecture` enforces by
            reading this file. Referencing it for packing alone would be a layer violation for a
            dependency that does not exist at run time or at compile time, so the file is named
            instead and the condition keeps a pack that happens before the tool was built from
            failing rather than from shipping something wrong.

            ⚠ The consequence, stated plainly because it is a real gap: `dotnet pack` on this project
            alone produces a package whose `tools/` is empty and whose `.targets` then falls through
            to its third answer and finds nothing. Packing has to follow a solution build. That is
            the same bargain `Vixen.Sdk` makes with the CLI it ships.

            ⚠ **The whole output directory and not the one assembly, and the difference is whether
            the step runs at all.** `Vixen.StyleGen.dll` on its own is an entry point with nothing
            behind it: the first line of `Main` touches `Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities`, and the shipped
            package placed neither that nor `Vixen.Core`, `Vixen.Ui.Styling` or the `.deps.json` that
            names them. Every consumer of every package produced so far would have got
            `FileNotFoundException: Could not load file or assembly 'Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities'` out
            of an `Exec` — checked by extracting a produced `.nupkg` and running it, which is the
            only way this was ever going to be found. A framework-dependent build is *flat*, so the
            fix is the directory: `*.dll` beside the `.deps.json` is exactly the layout the host
            probes.

            ⚠ `.dll` rather than `**`, deliberately. The `.pdb` and `.xml` beside them are for
            debugging an assembly nobody debugs from here and would double the count for nothing;
            the two `.json` files are named individually because they are the two that are read.
        -->
        <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../Tools/Vixen.StyleGen/bin/$(Configuration)/net10.0/*.dll"
              Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../Tools/Vixen.StyleGen/bin/$(Configuration)/net10.0/Vixen.StyleGen.dll')"
              Pack="true"
              PackagePath="tools/"
              Visible="false" />

        <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../Tools/Vixen.StyleGen/bin/$(Configuration)/net10.0/Vixen.StyleGen.deps.json"
              Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../Tools/Vixen.StyleGen/bin/$(Configuration)/net10.0/Vixen.StyleGen.deps.json')"
              Pack="true"
              PackagePath="tools/"
              Visible="false" />

        <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../Tools/Vixen.StyleGen/bin/$(Configuration)/net10.0/Vixen.StyleGen.runtimeconfig.json"
              Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../Tools/Vixen.StyleGen/bin/$(Configuration)/net10.0/Vixen.StyleGen.runtimeconfig.json')"
              Pack="true"
              PackagePath="tools/"
              Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ **The engine's default design tokens, embedded rather than globbed.** Every other
            project in the tree gets its .vcss files embedded by the glob in
            `Core/Vixen.Ui/build/Vixen.Ui.targets`, and this one cannot import that file: it sits
            *below* Vixen.Ui and referencing its build assets would invert the dependency for the
            sake of one line. Named here instead, with the same logical name the glob would have
            given it — RootNamespace plus the folder — which is what `ThemeTokens.DefaultSheet`
            asks for.
        -->
        <EmbeddedResource Include="Theme/vixen.default.vcss" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core\Vixen.Core.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Ui.Styling\Vixen.Ui.Styling.csproj" />

        <!--
            ⚠ **Vixen.Core.Yaml is gone, and this note used to say that made a generator possible.
            It was asked and answered in 2026-08: the step stays a process, and the reason is no
            longer about dependencies.** `ThemeTokens.Parse` read a `vixen.ui.yaml`; under `@theme` a
            token is a custom property in a stylesheet and the reader is a text scan over this
            assembly's own code, so there is no package reference here at all any more. That did
            retire the "an analyzer's dependencies do not travel with it" argument — and retiring it
            changed nothing, because two other blockers were behind it and the measurements went the
            other way regardless.

            This assembly is net10.0 and an analyzer must be netstandard, so a generator would have to
            *link* these 5 995 lines and compile them under a second language surface. RS1035 bans
            `System.IO` inside an analyzer, so it could not write the sheet or the report. And the
            arithmetic: the process launch a generator removes is ~52 ms, once per build that changed
            a scanned file and zero on a build that changed nothing, against ~20 ms of
            `UtilityGenerator.Generate` that a generator would pay *per keystroke* — the candidate set
            is deliberately over-inclusive, so it changes on nearly every edit and never caches.

            The workings are in `Tools/Vixen.StyleGen/README.md`, which also records what would
            reverse the decision. Note that the expiry of the YamlDotNet argument is not on that list;
            it is what prompted the measurement, and the measurement is the answer.
        -->
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
